  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show high \npressure centered over southern Lake Michigan to northern Illinois\nearly this afternoon, bringing quiet conditions across northeast \nWisconsin. High clouds continue to stream in from the northwest \nahead of thunderstorm activity over the northern Plains. Upstream,\na cold front stretches from northern Minnesota south-\nsouthwestward into eastern Colorado. High pressure will shift to \nthe eastern Great Lakes tonight, allowing the cold front to move \ninto northwest Wisconsin late tonight and traverse the area on \nTuesday.\n\nThe main weather focus in the short term is the cold frontal \npassage on Tuesday. High pressure shifting east tonight will \nmaintain dry conditions through early Tuesday morning, though high\nclouds will gradually thicken. The cold front will enter \nnorthwest Wisconsin after 4 AM Tuesday, with post-frontal showers\nreaching north-central Wisconsin after 7 AM. Convergence along \nthe front appears relatively weak as it moves through during the \nlate morning and afternoon. Forecast soundings indicate a stout \ncapping inversion in the 800-700 mb layer that will suppress \ndeeper convection for much of the day, keeping most activity in \nthe form of post-frontal showers along the boundary. As the cap \nbegins to erode late Tuesday afternoon into the evening, some \nconvective- allowing models produce widely scattered thunderstorms\nacross northeast Wisconsin. If storms develop, convective \nparameters look modest with skinny CAPE profiles of 500-1000 J/kg \nand deep-layer shear peaking at 20-25 kts, keeping the threat of \nsevere weather low.\n\nBy Tuesday evening, the frontal boundary is expected to become\nmore active from Iowa into southern Wisconsin within a developing\nfrontogenesis zone. Precipitation is forecast to track northeast\nalong the front overnight into Wednesday. Latest guidance over the\npast 24 hours has trended further south with the heaviest rainfall\naxis, keeping the significant precipitation across southern\nWisconsin. Consequently, rain chances have been lowered to 20 to\n35 percent across east-central Wisconsin on Wednesday, with dry\nconditions expected elsewhere.\n\nA slow-moving, flattened upper trough will slide across the region\nThursday through Friday. Thursday will remain mostly quiet before\nthe next cold front approaches late on Friday, increasing the\nprobability of showers and storms Friday afternoon and night. This\nfront will serve as the focus for periodic shower and thunderstorm\nchances through the weekend, though confidence in exact timing\nfrom Friday through Sunday remains low. Model guidance has trended\nwarmer and more unstable for Saturday and Sunday as the upper\ntrough lifts north and mid-level heights rebound, allowing heat\nand humidity from the western ridge to push into northeast\nWisconsin. These trends will be monitored for severe potential\nover the weekend, though deep-layer shear currently appears\nrelatively weak.",
